~A. The author uses really good words to make the scene super sad.#Incorrect. This keeps vague and informal language. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. The author uses mournful diction to create a sorrowful mood.#Correct. The revision is precise, academic, and concise. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. The author has words and sadness in the scene.#Incorrect. This is still vague. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. The author writes the scene and it is sad and good.#Incorrect. This lacks precision. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U05_UA_Q40::
Question ID: ENG3_U05_UA_Q40
[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: The evidence supports the claim, it is convincing. Which revision is correct and polished? \{
~A. The evidence supports the claim it is convincing.#Incorrect. This is a run-on. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. The evidence, supports the claim, it is convincing.#Incorrect. The commas interrupt the sentence incorrectly. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The evidence supports, the claim, it is convincing.#Incorrect. This punctuation is incorrect. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
